    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Salt Valley anticline is an asymmetric salt-cored anticline at the NW extent of the Paradox Basin in SEUtah. The anticline’s asymmetry is reflected by an asymmetry of fracture distribution on the flanks of the anticline. On the West flank there are deformation bands, joints, and faulted-joints in the Moab member of Entrada Sandstone, and faults (> l0 m offset) nucleated along zones of deformation bands. On the East flank, from Fiery Furnace to N of Devils Garden,there are joints and faulted-joints in the Moab member,and faults with greater than l0 m of offset localized along zones of joints. The sequence of fracturing in Entrada sandstone illustrates the hereditary nature of fracturing processes: the presence of one type of structure (e.g., zones of joints) helps nucleate and guide later structures (faults). The data indicate that one cannot explain joint orientations using simple fold-fracture models.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
